  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates the beneficial roles of underground fungal hyphae associated with crop roots, shown here connecting the root systems of two maize plants. A web-like hyphal network spreads through the soil and links the plants below ground, emphasizing that these fungi act as an interface between roots, soil, and surrounding microbes.\n\nThe main message is that this fungal network supports plant performance and soil health in several ways at once. It appears to improve the uptake and transfer of water and nutrients to the plants, while also mediating interactions between plants and the broader microbiota. The connection between the two root systems suggests that the hyphae can serve as a rapid channel for plant signal transmission, allowing communication across the underground network.\n\nThe figure also highlights protective and ecosystem-level effects: the fungal association seems to suppress soil-borne pathogens, strengthen plant resistance, and ultimately promote agricultural production and ecosystem stability. In addition, the network is shown as influencing longer-term soil processes, including carbon storage and changes in soil structure. Overall, the image presents fungal hyphae as a central below-ground system that links plant nutrition, defense, communication, and soil functioning.",
